  <p class="mb-4">
    A room full of WiFi that nobody can connect to. Small boards whisper to each other all night, like insomniac
    neighbours through a thin wall. When someone walks in, their body bends the conversation into audio and visual.
  </p>

  <p class="mb-4">
    In another timeline, the artist, Wasawo Somnus, turns the room into a company and sells it to the
    techno-military conglomerate, which is how these stories tend to end; until, years later, hacktivists send a
    worm in after it.
  </p>

  <p class="mb-4">
    The timeline unfolds with business cards, four screens, merger papers, and a game. The room goes on counting
    whoever stands in it, patiently, the way rooms do.
  </p>

  <h2 class="kicker art-kicker mt-8 mb-4">The installation</h2>

  <p class="mb-4">
    Six ESP32 boards broadcast WiFi signals across the room. As each transmission crosses it, a human body bends
    it, and the installation reads this interference as presence, movement and position. It uses no camera and no
    microphone. The visitor is detected not as an image but as a disturbance in a field, and this disturbance is
    rendered as sound and image in real time.
  </p>

  <p class="mb-4">
    Across four screens, the same signal is read four ways: as a falling field of raw data; as a company
    simulation that grows from the number of people in the room; as the share price of XYZA Inc., a fictional
    conglomerate that will acquire the artist's company, FireWire Corporation, in 2036; and as a 3D map of the
    room in which the visitor's position appears only as an estimate.
  </p>

  <p class="mb-4">
    WiFi sensing is a real and expanding technology, already embedded in the routers of homes, hotels and clinics.
    The work treats it as both an instrument and a near future. It asks what happens when ambient infrastructure
    becomes a means of counting bodies, and who profits when it does.
  </p>

  <h2 class="kicker art-kicker mt-8 mb-4">The fiction</h2>

  <p class="mb-4">
    The installation sets the audiovisual piece beside a speculative fiction of how this could turn out, told the
    way a company tells its own history: in objects and in paperwork. The four pieces in the room run along one
    line through time.
  </p>

  <ol class="mb-4 space-y-1">
    <li><strong>"Let's see Wasawo Somnus's card".</strong> Business card, past to present. The artist becomes the founder of FireWire Corporation, a record label first, and then a presence-sensing company.</li>
    <li><strong>Will we see through the future, or hear it coming?</strong> Main piece, present to future. Four screens and four speakers in the middle of the room.</li>
    <li><strong>Due Diligence Won't Be Forever Permanent.</strong> M&amp;A papers, 2036. Seven documents show the company being sold to XYZA Inc. for US$ 1.48bn.</li>
    <li><strong>worm.agent (or How I Learned to Stop Worrying and Love the LLM).</strong> Game, 2046. Hacktivists send a worm into XYZA's network to destroy the mainframe. Pick up the controller and you are the worm.</li>
  </ol>

  <p class="mb-4">
    The game is wired to the room. XYZA's stock rises with every body that walks into the gallery, and falls with
    every enemy the worm takes down.
  </p>

  <p class="mb-4">
    The full documentation, with diagrams, the seven papers and credits, is at
    <a href="https://www.seethroughfuture.xyz/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">seethroughfuture.xyz</a>.
  </p>
